<title>USA: Troy, N.Y., seen keeping property taxes flat - paper.</title>
<headline>Troy, N.Y., seen keeping property taxes flat - paper.</headline>
<dateline>NEW YORK 1996-10-01</dateline>
<text>
<p>The new budget for Troy, N.Y., which recently reached a last-minute refinancing pact with its bondholders, is not expected to raise property taxes or require lay-offs, the Albany Times Union said Tuesday.</p>
<p>Mayor Mark Pattison, whose budget was due on October 1, still is finalizing his budget, but he told the paper the budget will have no growth.</p>
<p>The city's financial plan is expected to be adopted over the next several weeks.</p>
<p>During his first nine months in office, Pattison got the city council to agree to a 21 percent increase in property taxes and obtained what the paper called &quot;significant concessions&quot; from union employees.</p>
<p>--U.S. Municipal Desk, 212-859-1650</p>
</text>
